#### Article 37

Regional Government shall undertake efforts to improve family quality as referred to in Article 36 paragraph (1) letter a including:  
a. guidance;  
b. counseling;  
c. supervision;  
d. facilitation of family development implementation;  
e. improving the quality of health of pregnant and breastfeeding mothers;

#### Article 38

Guidance as referred to in Article 37 letter a, includes:  
a. improving the quality of children by providing access to information, education, counseling and services regarding child care, parenting and child development through child protection programs and parenting pattern development;  
b. improving the quality of adolescents by providing access to information, education, counseling and services regarding family life through planned generation activities, youth information and counseling centers and adolescent family development;  
c. improving the quality of life of the elderly through elderly guidance programs, elderly family development, elderly guidance and counseling; and  
d. improving family economy.

#### Article 39

Counseling as referred to in Article 37 letter b, includes:  
a. providing protection and assistance to develop oneself through improving family capabilities and skills;  
b. conducting counseling on improving women's quality of life;  
c. conducting counseling on improving gender equality in family and community life;  
d. increasing access and opportunities to receive information and economic resources through efforts to increase prosperous family income and increase women's economic productivity; and  
e. undertaking empowerment efforts for female heads of household.

#### Article 40

Supervision as referred to in Article 37 letter c, includes:  
a. conducting evaluation and monitoring of child protection programs and parenting pattern development;  
b. conducting evaluation and monitoring of adolescent quality improvement;  
c. conducting evaluation and monitoring of elderly quality of life improvement;  
d. conducting evaluation and monitoring of family economic improvement; and  
e. conducting evaluation and monitoring of family capability and skill improvement.

#### Article 41

Provision of services for families as referred to in Article 36 paragraph (1) letter b includes:  
a. communication, information and education;  
b. contraceptive devices and drugs;  
c. recording and reporting of family planning services;  
d. adequate health service facilities and infrastructure; and  
e. breastfeeding corner rooms in every public facility.

#### Article 42

Communication, information and education as referred to in Article 41 letter a constitutes communication activities to increase knowledge and improve the attitudes and behavior of families and communities in population and family planning programs.

#### Article 43

1. In addition to the Regional Government, private parties are obligated to provide breastfeeding corner rooms.  
2. Breastfeeding corner rooms as referred to in paragraph (1) constitute proper and adequate rooms.  
3. Private parties that do not provide breastfeeding corner rooms as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:  
a. written warning;  
b. limitation of business activities;  
c. suspension of business activities;  
d. administrative fine; and/ore. revocation of permit.

# CHAPTER VI

## CHILD PROTECTION

#### Article 44

1. Regional Apparatus and related agencies are obligated to conduct gender and child data management which includes stages:  
a. collection;  
b. processing;  
c. analysis; and  
d. presentation.  
2. Data collection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is conducted through surveys, registration, routine agency statistics, research using secondary data, and/or other methods in accordance with the development of science and technology.  
3. Data processing as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b is conducted on all types of disaggregated data related to gender and children.  
4. Data analysis as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c is conducted using data processing methodology in accordance with needs.  
5. Presentation as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d is conducted using print media and/or electronic media.

#### Article 45

1. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection is obligated to administer gender and child data.  
2. Administration of gender and child data as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented in coordination with the Central Statistics Agency and the Agency that administers Governmental Affairs in the field of Regional Development Planning.

#### Article 46

1. Gender and child data as referred to in Article 44 paragraph (1) becomes material for data recapitulation by Regional Apparatus that administers Governmental Affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.  
2. Recapitulation results as referred to in paragraph (1) are reported to the Regent every current year and disseminated to Regional Apparatus and related agencies.

# CHAPTER VII

## FULFILLMENT OF CHILDREN'S RIGHTS

## Part One

General

#### Article 47

1. Regional Government in efforts to fulfill children's rights is responsible for:  
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the fulfillment of children's rights;  
b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status of the child, birth order of the child, and physical and/or mental condition;  
c. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for improving family quality in realizing children's rights;  
d. guaranteeing the protection, care, and welfare of children with due regard to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons legally responsible for the child;  
e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of fulfillment of children's rights;  
f. guaranteeing the right of children to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the child;  
g. conducting supervision and guidance in the context of implementing the fulfillment of children's rights;  
h. conducting cooperation with private parties and/or NGOs in the implementation of child protection;  
2. Government responsibility as referred to in the implementation of Child Protection Institutions and Government Protection in the field of Child Empowerment.

## Part Two

Birth Registration and Education

#### Article 48

1. Regional Government is obligated to administer birth registration of children with the issuance of birth certificates.  
2. Birth certificates as referred to in paragraph (1) are administered free of charge.

#### Article 49

Regional Government guarantees the implementation of a 9 (nine) year compulsory education program for children aged 7 (seven) to 15 (fifteen) years.

#### Article 50

1. Every organizer of education units for children to obtain educational services without discrimination.  
2. Every organizer of education units is prohibited from expelling children from educational institutions unless there is a guarantee for the continuity of the child's education.  
3. In the event that a child victim of violence experiences dropping out of school, the organizer of the education unit must coordinate with related agencies.

#### Article 51

1. Children with disabilities and children with special needs are given equal opportunities to obtain formal, non-formal and informal educational services, including inclusive education programs.  
2. Regional government provides teaching staff for inclusive education at every education unit.

#### Article 52

Children in conflict with the law, children who experience pregnancy outside of marriage and/or unwanted pregnancy and child victims of HIV/AIDS transmission are protected in their rights to obtain education.

## Part Three

Health and Social

