#### Article 66

1. The Regional Action Plan for the Development of a Child-Friendly Regency is directed towards the fulfillment of children's rights divided into 5 (five) target groups, comprising:  
a. civil rights and freedoms;  
b. family environment and alternative care;  
c. basic health and welfare;  
d. education, leisure time utilization, and cultural activities; and  
e. special protection.  
2. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan for the Development of a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.  
3. In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of the Regional Action Plan for the Development of a Child-Friendly Regency in the Region, a Child-Friendly Regency Task Force shall be established.  
4. The establishment and membership composition of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force shall be stipulated by Regent Decree.  
5. The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force shall be domiciled at the OPD that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.  
6. The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) shall have the principal duties of:  
a. coordinating the implementation of policies and development of a Child-Friendly Regency;  
b. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication, information and education on Child-Friendly Regency policies;  
c. collecting baseline data for the development of a Child-Friendly Regency;  
d. conducting needs analysis sourced from baseline data;  
e. disseminating baseline data;  
f. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing a Child-Friendly Regency, adjusted to regional potential;  
g. preparing a 5 (five) year regional action plan for a Child-Friendly Regency and work mechanisms; and  
h. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once (1) a year.

#### Article 68

In carrying out its duties, the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force may coordinate and cooperate with P2TP2A institutions and other institutions that provide child protection services.

# CHAPTER VIII

## GENDER AND CHILD DATA SYSTEM

#### Article 69

The Regional Government, in order to provide protection for children:  
a. prevents violence against children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;  
b. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling on child protection and rights;  
c. develops child-friendly school models;  
d. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for children;  
e. compiles databases and information on acts of violence against children;  
f. maps locations and areas prone to violence against children;  
g. coordinates and cooperates in preventing violations of children's rights;  
h. strengthens the function of institutions or organizations engaged in child protection; and  
i. encourages public awareness of the importance of child protection by establishing women and child protection assistants.

#### Article 70

Service efforts undertaken by the Regional Government towards women victims of violence as referred to in Article 20 through Article 33 shall apply mutatis mutandis to service efforts for women victims of violence.

# CHAPTER IX

## PARTICIPATION OF THE COMMUNITY, AND PARENTS AND/OR FAMILY

## Part One

Community

#### Article 71

1. Community participation in efforts to protect women and children:  
a. participates in prevention and provides support for the development of programs to prevent violations of the rights of women and children;  
b. provides protection to victims and emergency assistance;  
c. provides information from/or reports violations of the rights of women and children to the competent authorities; and  
d. monitors the law enforcement process for cases of violations of the rights of women and children.  
2. Families and Parents are responsible for protecting and preventing violations of the rights of women and children.  
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be based on laws and regulations and the values of local wisdom of the local community.

#### Article 72

1. In order to prevent violations of women's rights, the community:  
a. monitors various conditions occurring in its environment that may give rise to violations of women's rights;  
b. increases awareness of violations of women's rights;  
c. strengthens the role of community, religious and family environment organizations; and  
d. prevents unregistered marriages.  
2. In order to prevent violations of children's rights, the community:  
a. monitors various conditions occurring in its environment that give rise to violations of children's rights;  
b. increases awareness of violations of children's rights;  
c. strengthens the role of community, religious and family environment organizations; and  
d. prevents early marriage.

## Part Two

Parents and/or Family

#### Article 73

Families in efforts to protect women's rights shall play a role in:  
a. providing equal opportunities for women to develop themselves in the fields of education, social, economic, political and cultural affairs;  
b. preventing violations of women's rights within the household; and  
c. protecting women from conflict and domestic violence that degrades the dignity of women.

#### Article 74

1. Parents in efforts to protect children's rights shall play a role in:  
a. caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting children;  
b. developing children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;  
c. providing formal and non-formal education;  
d. guiding children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and  
e. preventing early marriage.  
2. In the event that parents are absent, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to any reason, are unable to carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

# CHAPTER X

## GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

#### Article 75

1. Guidance on women's empowerment and child protection shall be conducted by the Regional Government.  
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted in the form of:  
a. providing training for government officials and law enforcers on the prevention and handling of violence;  
b. providing training for religious leaders, community leaders, customary leaders, educators, journalists and media managers in order to prevent violence against women and children;  
c. providing training to health workers who provide health services to women and children victims of violence at community health centers, integrated service units and general hospitals;  
d. providing training to social rehabilitation officers and spiritual guidance officers in order to protect women and children victims of violence; and  
e. improving the professionalism of officers, managers of violence case data against women and children at community health centers, integrated service units and hospitals.  
3. The Regent has the authority to conduct guidance in the administration of women's empowerment and child protection.

#### Article 76

1. The Head of the PD that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection shall submit a report on the implementation of women and child protection programs to the Regent.  
2. The report as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be submitted periodically once (1) every 6 (six) months.

#### Article 77

1. The Regent shall conduct supervision and evaluation of the administration of women and child protection periodically once (1) every 3 (three) months.  
2. In conducting supervision and evaluation as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent may appoint a PD in accordance with its duties and functions.  
3. The results of supervision and evaluation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall constitute evaluation material for the policy of women and child protection programs in the Region.
